@meta
id: 02-15
title: The branch undo cannot reach
chapter: 02
type: lesson
skills: undo-branches, undo, redo
par: 50
minutes: 8
vscode: impossible — the work is gone
open: pricing.md

@brief
Every editor you have used keeps undo as a straight line. Go back three
steps, type one new character, and the three steps you undid are
destroyed. Vim does not destroy them. When you make a change from a point
in the past, it starts a *branch*, and the old path is still there,
sitting beside the new one.

Which means `u` and `Ctrl-R` are not enough. They only walk the current
branch. To move between branches you need the other pair: `g-` and `g+`,
which step backwards and forwards through every state in the order it was
created, branch or no branch.

Here is the trap, and you have to walk into it deliberately for the lesson
to land. In order:

  1. Append ` (per order)` to the Flat rate line. `Esc`.

  2. Change your mind. `u`.

  3. Append ` (customers only)` to the Free over line. `Esc`.

  4. Pricing come back: step 1 was right and step 3 was wrong. You want
     the file with `(per order)` on the flat rate line and nothing on the
     free over line.

Get there and save. Read the last two keys of `@keys` before you start,
and do not retype anything.

@keys
| `g-` | go back one state in time, across branches |
| `g+` | go forward one state in time, across branches |
| `:undolist` | every state, its number, and when it was made |
| `:undo 1` | jump straight to state number 1 |
| `u` `Ctrl-R` | undo and redo along the current branch only |

@hints
Try `u` and `Ctrl-R` first and watch them fail. `u` takes you to the
original file; `Ctrl-R` brings back the change you just made, not the one
you undid. Neither can see the other branch.
--
The three states exist in the order you made them: original, then the
flat rate version, then the free over version. You are standing on the
third. You want the second. `g-` moves one step back through that list
regardless of which branch each state is on.
--
One `g-`, then `ZZ`. If you want to see the map first, `:undolist` prints
the states and `:undo 1` jumps to the numbered one directly.

@solution
    jj $ a  (per order) <Esc>
    u
    j $ a  (customers only) <Esc>
    g-
    ZZ

Forty-three keystrokes, two of which did something no other editor you
have used can do.

Work out why `u` cannot get you there. After step 2 the history is a line
with two states: the original and the flat rate version, and you are
standing on the original. Step 3 branches off the original, so now the
original has two children. `u` from there goes to the parent — the
original. `Ctrl-R` goes back down the branch you last used — the free over
version. The flat rate version is one node away the whole time and
completely unreachable by those two keys.

`g-` ignores the shape and uses time. Every state vim has ever held for
this buffer gets a sequence number when it is created, and `g-` walks
that numbering downwards, `g+` upwards. So `g-` from state 2 lands on
state 1 no matter where either of them sits in the tree.

`:undolist` shows you the numbering when you are lost:

    number changes  when               saved
         1       1  10:41:02
         2       1  10:41:19

Read it, pick a number, `:undo 2`. That is the whole interface.

You will not use this weekly. You will use it on the day you undo further
than you meant to, type something, and feel the floor go. Nothing is
gone. `g-` a few times, or `:undolist` and jump. Combined with
`set undofile` from the last lesson, work you abandoned last Tuesday is
still recoverable this Thursday, which is a genuinely unreasonable thing
for a text editor to offer.

@start pricing.md
# Shipping rules

- Flat rate 4.99
- Free over 50
- Express is 9.99

@expected pricing.md
# Shipping rules

- Flat rate 4.99 (per order)
- Free over 50
- Express is 9.99
